WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.380
HTML encoding is used when data is rendered as text in an HTML body context.

2
00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:19.000
Special characters such as less-than, greater-than, ampersand, and sometimes quotes are represented so the browser displays them as text instead of treating them as markup.

3
00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:27.340
If a user enters text that looks like a tag, HTML encoding should make the browser show the characters rather than create an element.

4
00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:30.400
That distinction is the heart of output encoding.

5
00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:36.840
Modern frameworks often auto-escape template output in common HTML text contexts.

6
00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:41.420
This is valuable because it makes safe behavior the default for ordinary variable insertion.

7
00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:45.200
However, auto-escaping is not a reason to stop thinking.

8
00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:59.380
Developers can accidentally bypass it by using raw HTML helpers, unsafe template directives, manual string concatenation, direct DOM assignment to dangerous properties, or server-rendered content that the framework does not understand.

9
00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:04.440
Auto-escaping helps most when developers stay inside the safe patterns it provides.

10
00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:09.200
HTML encoding is not the same as sanitizing rich HTML.

11
00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:20.500
If the application allows users to submit formatted content, such as markdown or rich text, the system may need a sanitizer that permits a controlled subset of tags and attributes.

12
00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:26.120
That is a separate design decision and should use mature libraries with conservative configuration.

13
00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:31.100
For ordinary text, prefer encoding and safe template insertion.

14
00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:36.560
For rich content, sanitize before rendering and still review the final context carefully.
